{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>He will fill your hands.</b> The word 'end' is missing, as if it said 'for the end of seven days,' or 'the time of.' Some say: you shall not go out seven days, but at night they would go out for their needs. The correct view in my eyes is that they went out in time of need, by day and by night. One great sage said that they dug a place in the court of the Tent of Meeting — this is far-fetched. And Scripture says, 'And the children of Israel wept for Moses thirty days' (Deuteronomy 34:8), as if there was not a moment when they were not weeping; rather, Scripture spoke of their sitting at the entrance of the Tent of Meeting day and night, the meaning being that they would not engage in any matter and would not go to another place. Likewise, 'he shall not go out from the sanctuary' (Leviticus 21:12), as I will explain."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "Resolved 'ובלילה היו יוצאים לצרכם' as referring to bodily needs; the phrase is standard rabbinic idiom but left without gloss per terseness rules."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:09:10.149195774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}